{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Hass1967-AR3-Ge-Si-CeO2-MgF2",
  "Source": "G. Hass and E. Ritter, \"Optical film materials and their applications,\" J. Vac. Sci. Technol. 4, 71-79 (1967). doi:10.1116/1.1492525 - Fig. 7 and caption, p. 75 ('Ge plate with triple-layer antireflection coatings of Si + CeO2 + MgF2. All layers lambda/4 thick at lambda = 3.5 mu'); indices from Fig. 5 chart, p. 74",
  "Notes": "Triple-layer infrared antireflection coating on germanium (2-7 um), all layers quarter-wave at 3.5 um\nSpecification: Raise the transmittance of a Ge plate (both faces coated) from about 47 % to above 90 % over 2-7 um.\nReported: Fig. 7 (measured transmittance of the plate, coated on both sides): coated T about 82 % at 2.0 um, peak about 97 % at 2.35 um, about 95 % at 2.6-2.7 um, a dip to about 87 % at 2.95 um (likely water absorption in the films), about 93-94 % at 3.2-3.5 um, rising to about 97-98 % at 5.0-5.5 um, then falling to about 92 % at 6 um, 86 % at 6.5 um and 84 % at 7 um. Uncoated plate T about 47 % flat over 2-7 um. Values read from the plot.\nNotes: Caption lists the layers from the Ge outward (Si + CeO2 + MgF2); reversed here so layer 1 = MgF2 next to air. Indices are the infrared values printed in Fig. 5 (labels placed near 2 um): Ge 4.0, Si 3.3, CeO2 2.2, MgF2 1.35; taken as non-dispersive and lossless. The measured curve is the transmittance of the whole plate: the uncoated value of 47 % equals 2-surface incoherent transmittance of n = 4.0 ((1-R)/(1+R) with R = 0.36), so the plate is assumed coated identically on BOTH faces with incoherent (thick) Ge; the Ge thickness is not given and absorption in Ge is neglected. To compare with Fig. 7 use a back surface carrying the mirror image of the same stack. Normal incidence assumed. Ideal QW admittance gives R of about 0.02 % per face at 3.5 um.\nLayers from Fig. 7 and caption, p. 75 ('Ge plate with triple-layer antireflection coatings of Si + CeO2 + MgF2. All layers lambda/4 thick at lambda = 3.5 mu'); indices from Fig. 5 chart, p. 74; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours T 76.4 % at 2, 100 % at 3.5, 99.8 % at 5, 97.0 % at 7 um (both faces coated, thick plate); paper measured (Fig. 7): ~82, 93-94, 97-98, 84 %.",
